	url = {http://rgdoi.net/10.13140/RG.2.2.36483.81445},
	doi = {10.13140/RG.2.2.36483.81445},
	abstract = {The role of public policies, including those relating to forest resources, is to change the behavior of users and managers in a short period less than fifteen years. Until 1995, Madagascar has never had an objective public policy that can help manage forest resources according to institutional standards and therefore precise objective. After the publication, in 1997, of a forest policy welcomed by all institutional actors as the reference, no significant change has yet been felt in the management and use of forests. The process of forest degradation has not been halted because deforestation is ongoing (although attenuated), the forest seems not better managed than before, the area and forest potential has not increased as desired and economic performance of the forest has not increased as wanted.},
